"(PDF) Joint Image Registration and Fusion for Panchromatic and Multispectral Images (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265856030_Joint_Image_Registration_and_Fusion_for_Panchromatic_and_Multispectral_Images)\nciteturn153view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265856030_Joint_Image_Registration_and_Fusion_for_Panchromatic_and_Multispectral_Images\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1451\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: # Joint Image Registration and Fusion for Panchromatic and Multispectral Images\nL2: \nL3:   * March 2015\nL4:   * cite0†IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters 12(3):467-471\nL5: \nL6: DOI:cite1†10.1109/LGRS.2014.2346398†doi.org L7: \nL8: Authors:\nL9: \nL10: cite2†Qian Zhang L11: \nL12:   * cite3†Shenzhen University L13: \nL14: cite4†Zhi-Guo Cao L15: \nL16:   * cite5†Huazhong University of Science and Technology L17: \nL18: cite6†Zhongwen Hu L19: \nL20:   * cite3†Shenzhen University L21: \nL22: cite7†Yonghong Jia L23: \nL24: cite7†Yonghong Jia L25: \nL26:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL27: Show all 5 authors Hide\nL28: \nL29: cite8†Image†i1.rgstatic.net L30: \nL31: cite9†Download full-text PDF cite10†Read full-text L32: ## Abstract and Figures\nL33: Both image registration and fusion are essential steps to produce high-resolution multispectral images in remote sensing. Traditionally, they are viewed as two independent processes. As a result, the registration errors ignored in the fusion process can significantly affect the fusion quality. In this context, an iterative optimization approach, which jointly considers the registration and fusion processes, is proposed for panchromatic (PAN) and multispectral (MS) images.\nL34: Given a registration method and a fusion method, the joint optimization process is described as finding the optimal registration parameters to gain the optimal fusion performance. In our approach, the downhill simplex algorithm is adopted to refine the registration parameters iteratively. Experiments on a set of PAN and MS images of ZY-3 and GeoEye-1 show that the proposed approach outperforms several competing ones in terms of registration accuracy and fusion quality.\nL35: cite11†Workflow of the proposed approach.…  L36: cite12†Source images, registered images, and fused results of proposed approach for two data sets (displayed in a color-infrared manner). (a) ZY-3 PAN image, 1024 × 1024 pixels. (b) ZY-3 MS image, 745 × 747 pixels. (c) Registered image of JPSF for ZY-3 data set. (d) Fused image of JPSF for ZY-3 data set. (e) GeoEye-1 PAN image, 1024 × 1024 pixels. (f) GeoEye-1 MS image, 503 × 460 pixels. (g) Registered image of JPSF for GeoEye-1 data set. (h) Fused image of JPSF for GeoEye-1 data set.…  L37: Figures - uploaded by cite2†Qian Zhang L38: \nL39: Author content\nL40: \nL41: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Qian Zhang\nL42: \nL43: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL44: \nL45: cite13†Image: ResearchGate Logo L46: \nL47: Discover the world's research\nL48: \nL49:   * 25+ million members\nL50:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL51:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL52: cite14†Join for free L53: \nL54: Public Full-text 1\nL55: \nL56: Content uploaded by cite2†Qian Zhang L57: \nL58: Author content\nL59: \nL60: All content in this area was uploaded by Qian Zhang on Nov 04, 2014\nL61: \nL62: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL63: IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 3,MARCH 2015 467\nL64: \nL65: Joint Image Registration and Fusion for\nL66: \nL67: Panchromatic and Multispectral Images\nL68: \nL69: Qian Zhang, Zhiguo Cao, Zhongwen Hu, Yonghong Jia, and Xiaoliang Wu\nL70: \nL71: Abstract—Both image registration and fusion are essential steps\nL72: \nL73: to produce high-resolution multispectral images in remote sensing.\nL74: \nL75: Traditionally, they are viewed as two independent processes. As\nL76: \nL77: a result, the registration errors ignored in the fusion process can\nL78: signiﬁcantly affect the fusion quality. In this context, an iterative\nL79: \nL80: optimization approach, which jointly considers the registration\nL81: \nL82: and fusion processes, is proposed for panchromatic (PAN) and\nL83: \nL84: multispectral (MS) images. Given a registration method and a\nL85: \nL86: fusion method, the joint optimization process is described as ﬁnd-\nL87: \nL88: ing the optimal registration parameters to gain the optimal fusion\nL89: \nL90: performance. In our approach, the downhill simplex algorithm is\nL91: adopted to reﬁne the registration parameters iteratively. Experi-\nL92: \nL93: ments on a set of PAN and MS images of ZY-3 and GeoEye-1 show\nL94: \nL95: that the proposed approach outperforms several competing ones\nL96: \nL97: in terms of registration accuracy and fusion quality.\nL98: \nL99: Index Terms—Image fusion, image fusion quality, image regis-\nL100: \nL101: tration, iterative optimization, registration accuracy.\nL102: \nL103: I. I NTRODUCTION\nL104: \nL105: I MAGE registration is a crucial step in many remote sens-\nL106: \nL107: ing applications, such as image fusion, change detection,\nL108: image mosaicing, and map updating [1]. In general, registra-\nL109: \nL110: tion methods can be divided into two categories: area-based\nL111: \nL112: and feature-based methods. In area-based methods, the image\nL113: \nL114: intensity of corresponding regions is used for matching, while\nL115: \nL116: in feature-based methods, features on points, lines, and regions\nL117: \nL118: are used. In remote sensing, area-based methods are sensitive to\nL119: \nL120: the intensity changes and local distortions of satellite images.\nL121: \nL122: Due to the limitation of area-based methods, feature-based\nL123: methods are commonly used for remote sensing image regis-\nL124: \nL125: tration [2]. Recently, scale-invariant feature transform (SIFT)\nL126: \nL127: [3] is widely and successfully applied in remote sensing image\nL128: \nL129: registration, owing to its good characteristics of being invariant\nL130: \nL131: Manuscript received March 19, 2014; revised June 5, 2014 and July 6, 2014;\nL132: \nL133: accepted July 29, 2014. This work was supported in part by the National\nL134: \nL135: Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant 41001260 and in part by\nL136: the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities under Grant\nL137: \nL138: 201121302020003.\nL139: \nL140: Q. Zhang and Z. Cao are with the National Key Laboratory of Science\nL141: \nL142: and Technology on Multi-spectral Information Processing, School of Automa-\nL143: \nL144: tion, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan 430074, China\nL145: \nL146: (e-mail: hangfanzq@163.com; zgcao@hust.edu.cn).\nL147: \nL148: Z. Hu is with the Shenzhen Key Laboratory of Spatial Smart Sensing and Ser-\nL149: \nL150: vices, Mapping and GeoInformation, College of Civil Engineering, Shenzhen\nL151: University, Shenzhen 518060, China (e-mail: zhongwenhu@163.com).\nL152: \nL153: Y. Jia is with the School of Remote Sensing and Information Engineering,\nL154: \nL155: Wuhan University, Wuhan 430079, China (e-mail: yhjia2000@sina.com).\nL156: \nL157: X. Wu is with the Division of Mathematics, Informatics and Statistics,\nL158: \nL159: Commonwealth Scientiﬁc and Industrial Research Organization, North Ryde,\nL160: \nL161: N.S.W. 2113, Australia (e-mail: xiaoliang.wu@csiro.au).\nL162: \nL163: Color versions of one or more of the ﬁgures in this paper are available online\nL164: at http://ieeexplore.ieee.org.\nL165: \nL166: Digital Object Identiﬁer 10.1109/LGRS.2014.2346398\nL167: \nL168: to image scaling, rotation, change in illumination, and sensor\nL169: \nL170: viewpoint [4].\nL171: \nL172: Image fusion is a technique used to integrate the geometric\nL173: \nL174: detail of a high-resolution panchromatic (PAN) image and the\nL175: \nL176: color information of a low spatial resolution multispectral (MS)\nL177: \nL178: image to produce a high-resolution MS image [5]. There are\nL179: \nL180: two limitations of current fusion techniques: the distortion in\nL181: color and the performance instability to varied data sets. To\nL182: \nL183: reduce the color distortion and improve the fusion quality, a\nL184: \nL185: wide variety of fusion methods have been presented up to\nL186: \nL187: now. According to Aiazzi’s advices [6], these methods can be\nL188: \nL189: roughly categorized into the component substitution (CS) meth-\nL190: \nL191: ods and the multiresolution analysis methods by their protocol.\nL192: \nL193: Synthetic variable ratio (SVR) [7], as the representative fusion\nL194: \nL195: method of the CS technique, has been a popular method due\nL196: to its clear physical meaning and theoretical basis, particularly\nL197: \nL198: when it was commercialized in PCI Geomatica software [8].\nL199: \nL200: Although various algorithms have been proposed for image\nL201: \nL202: registration and image fusion, they are traditionally viewed as\nL203: \nL204: two independent processes. In addition, the quality of image\nL205: \nL206: registration and that of image fusion are also evaluated sepa-\nL207: \nL208: rately. The registration accuracy is often evaluated using the\nL209: \nL210: root mean square error (RMSE), while the fusion quality is usu-\nL211: ally assessed using image quality indexes between the original\nL212: \nL213: and the fused MS images, such as the correlation coefﬁcient\nL214: \nL215: (CC), relative global dimensional synthesis error (ERGAS)\nL216: \nL217: [9], and Q4 [10]. Due to the separation of the registration\nL218: \nL219: process and the fusion process, the fusion quality will be highly\nL220: \nL221: dependent on the registration results, and the registration errors\nL222: \nL223: ignored in the fusion process can signiﬁcantly affect the fusion\nL224: \nL225: quality [11], [12].\nL226: Chen et al. [13] presented a maximum likelihood approach to\nL227: \nL228: joint image registration and fusion. The approach is predicated\nL229: \nL230: on the assumption that the registration error should be limited\nL231: \nL232: within a certain range. Meanwhile, the speciﬁc fusion process\nL233: \nL234: of the approach is predicated on the assumption that images\nL235: \nL236: hold the same value within a certain window [13]. In this\nL237: \nL238: letter, a novel optimization approach, which considers jointly\nL239: \nL240: the registration and fusion processes, is proposed for PAN\nL241: and MS images. The advantages of the proposed approach are\nL242: \nL243: its robustness and simplicity. In our approach, the downhill\nL244: \nL245: simplex algorithm is employed to solve the joint optimization\nL246: \nL247: process, where the fusion quality is used as a measure to reﬁne\nL248: \nL249: the registration. It avoids the limit of the obvious geometric\nL250: \nL251: difference owing to its existing initial registration process, and\nL252: \nL253: it can employ a different registration and fusion method in the\nL254: \nL255: joint optimization framework.\nL256: The contributions of this work lie in two aspects: 1) The\nL257: \nL258: fusion quality is used to evaluate the registration accuracy,\nL259: \nL260: and 2) a novel joint image registration and fusion approach is\nL261: \nL262: 1545-598X © 2014 IEEE. Personal use is permitted, but republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission.\nL263: \nL264: See http://www.ieee.org/publications_standards/publications/rights/index.html for more information.\nL265: \nL266: 468 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 3, MARCH 2015\nL267: Fig. 1. Workﬂow of the proposed approach.\nL268: \nL269: proposed, in which the downhill simplex algorithm is adopted\nL270: \nL271: to reﬁne the registration parameters. The rest of this letter\nL272: \nL273: is organized as follows. The proposed approach is described\nL274: \nL275: in Section II, the experimental results and discussions are\nL276: \nL277: presented in Section III, and the conclusions are given in the\nL278: \nL279: last section.\nL280: \nL281: II. I TERATIVE O PTIMIZATION A PPROACH\nL282: \nL283: Fig. 1 shows the main steps of the proposed approach.\nL284: Given a registration method and a fusion method, the joint\nL285: \nL286: optimization process is described as ﬁnding the optimal reg-\nL287: \nL288: istration parameters to gain the optimal fusion performance.\nL289: \nL290: To achieve this goal, an iterative strategy is adopted. First,\nL291: \nL292: the registered result of the MS image is obtained according to\nL293: \nL294: the initial transformation parameters, which are estimated via\nL295: \nL296: image registration. Next, the fused image is produced, and the\nL297: \nL298: fusion quality is assessed for objective function construction.\nL299: Finally, the registration parameters are updated iteratively by\nL300: \nL301: examining the objective function.\nL302: \nL303: A. Transformation Parameter Estimation\nL304: \nL305: Given a reference image and a sensed image, the registration\nL306: \nL307: process aims to ﬁnd an optimal transformation between the two\nL308: \nL309: images. In this letter, let us consider the PAN image as the ref-\nL310: \nL311: erence image and the MS image as the sensed image. The trans-\nL312: \nL313: formation from MS image to PAN image can be represented as\nL314: \nL315: x=p 1+p 2 u+p 3 v\nL316: \nL317: y=p 4+p 5 u+p 6 v(1)\nL318: where (x, y)and (u, v)denote the pixel coordinates of the PAN\nL319: \nL320: and MS images, respectively, and the afﬁne transformation\nL321: \nL322: parameters P=(p 1,p 2,p 3,p 4,p 5,p 6).\nL323: \nL324: Once the matched features are detected from PAN and MS\nL325: \nL326: images, the transformation parameters are estimated by least\nL327: \nL328: squares solution using the afﬁne transformation model.\nL329: \nL330: B. Objective Function Construction\nL331: \nL332: In our approach, the fusion quality is used to evaluate the\nL333: \nL334: registration accuracy. When the reference image and the sensed\nL335: image are precisely matched, the fusion quality should be\nL336: \nL337: optimal. As the registration error increases, the fusion quality\nL338: \nL339: begins to decrease. Therefore, we construct an objective func-\nL340: \nL341: tion F to evaluate the registration accuracy using fusion quality.\nL342: \nL343: Inspired by Kim et al. [14], the joint optimization process\nL344: \nL345: of image registration and fusion process can be described as\nL346: \nL347: ﬁnding the transformation parameters P that minimize the\nL348: \nL349: objective function F. It can be deﬁned as\nL350: \nL351: ˆ\nL352: \nL353: P=argmin\nL354: \nL355: P F(A, B;P)(2)\nL356: where P represents the transformation parameters and A and B\nL357: \nL358: denote the PAN and MS images, respectively.\nL359: \nL360: The ERGAS index, which is considered as F, is chosen to\nL361: \nL362: evaluate the quality of the fused image, considering its running\nL363: \nL364: efﬁciency and its high sensitivity to registration errors. The\nL365: \nL366: ERGAS index is represented as [9]\nL367: \nL368: ERGAS = 100 h\nL369: \nL370: l\nL371: \nL372: \nL373: \nL374: \nL375: \nL376: \nL377: \nL378: 1\nL379: \nL380: N b\nL381: \nL382: N b\nL383: \nL384: \nL385: \nL386: i=1 RMSE 2\nL387: \nL388: F(B i)\nL389: \nL390: M 2\nL391: \nL392: i(3)\nL393: \nL394: where h is the resolution of the high-resolution PAN image, l\nL395: is the resolution of the low spatial resolution MS image, N b is\nL396: \nL397: the number of MS bands involved in the fusion, M i denotes\nL398: \nL399: the mean gray value of the i th band of the registered MS\nL400: \nL401: image, which is resampled to the size of the PAN image, and\nL402: \nL403: RMSE F(B i), computed in the following expression, represents\nL404: \nL405: the difference in spectral information between the i th band of\nL406: \nL407: the fused MS image and that of the registered MS image:\nL408: \nL409: RMSE F(B i)=\nL410: \nL411: \nL412: \nL413: \nL414: \nL415: \nL416: \nL417: 1\nL418: \nL419: WH\nL420: \nL421: W\nL422: \nL423: \nL424: \nL425: x=1\nL426: \nL427: H\nL428: \nL429: \nL430: \nL431: y=1\nL432: \nL433: [MS i(x, y )−Fused\nL434: i(x, y)]2\nL435: \nL436: (4)\nL437: \nL438: where W and H denote the width and the height of the fused\nL439: \nL440: image and MS i(x, y )and Fused\nL441: \nL442: i(x, y)represent the gray\nL443: \nL444: value of the i th band of the registered MS image and the fused\nL445: \nL446: image at the coordinate of (x, y), respectively.\nL447: \nL448: The lower the value of the ERGAS index, the better the global\nL449: \nL450: spectral quality of the fused images. When the ERGAS index\nL451: \nL452: obtains the minimum value, the corresponding transformation\nL453: \nL454: parameters are the solution of the joint optimization process.\nL455: C. Iterative Optimization\nL456: \nL457: In order to minimize the objective function, the downhill\nL458: \nL459: simplex method [15] is adopted in this letter. It is a technique for\nL460: \nL461: minimizing an objective function in a multidimensional space.\nL462: \nL463: It is started with N+1 points by deﬁning an initial simplex,\nL464: \nL465: which consists of an initial estimate of the parameter vector and\nL466: \nL467: N other vectors [15].\nL468: \nL469: In our approach, we ﬁrst construct the initial simplex by\nL470: \nL471: separating the initial parameter vector along six parameter axes\nL472: based on afﬁne transformation [16]\nL473: \nL474: P i=P 0+λn i,i=1,2,...,6(5)\nL475: \nL476: where P 0 represents the initial afﬁne transformation parame-\nL477: \nL478: ters, λ denotes a constant for the initial simplex, and n i’s are\nL479: \nL480: ZHANG et al.: JOINT IMAGE REGISTRATION AND FUSION FOR PANCHROMATIC AND MULTISPECTRAL IMAGES 469\nL481: \nL482: six vectors. Each parameter value of n i has a different property\nL483: \nL484: and scale due to the afﬁne transformation model.\nL485: \nL486: Then, the objective function value is calculated for every\nL487: point in a simplex. According to the function values, the method\nL488: \nL489: iteratively determines a new simplex based on the movement,\nL490: \nL491: reﬂection, expansion, and contraction steps, whose detailed\nL492: \nL493: explanation can be found in [15].\nL494: \nL495: The maximum number of iterations N it is set in the iterative\nL496: \nL497: optimization process. In addition, we set the terminating condi-\nL498: \nL499: tion of the optimization procedure as\nL500: \nL501: |F max −F min|\nL502: \nL503: |F max|+|F min |<ε (6)\nL504: \nL505: where F max and F min denote the maximum and the minimum\nL506: objective function value of points in the simplex, respectively,\nL507: \nL508: and ε is the precision tolerance.\nL509: \nL510: The detailed steps of the iterative optimization process are\nL511: \nL512: listed as follows:\nL513: \nL514: Algorithm 1 The Iterative Optimization Algorithm\nL515: \nL516: Input: initial afﬁne transformation parameters P 0.\nL517: \nL518: Output: reﬁned transformation parameters ˆ\nL519: \nL520: P.\nL521: \nL522: 1: Let n=0, and set the stop condition (including maximum\nL523: \nL524: iteration number N it and precision tolerance ε).\nL525: \nL526: 2: Construct the simplex P i=P 0+λn i,i =1,2,...,6.\nL527: 3: while n≤N it do\nL528: \nL529: 4: for each i∈[0,6] do\nL530: \nL531: 5: Transform the sensed image using P i. Produce the\nL532: \nL533: fused image.\nL534: \nL535: 6: Calculate the objective function F i by ERGAS index.\nL536: \nL537: 7: end for\nL538: \nL539: 8: Determine F max and F min. Find the index i min of F min .\nL540: \nL541: 9: if (|F max −F min|/|F max |+|F min|)<ε do\nL542: \nL543: 10: Set ˆ\nL544: \nL545: P=P i min . Break\nL546: \nL547: 11: end if\nL548: \nL549: 12: Determine the new simplex P i via movement,r eﬂection,\nL550: \nL551: expansion, and contraction steps.\nL552: \nL553: 13: n=n+1.\nL554: \nL555: 14: end while\nL556: \nL557: III. E XPERIMENTS AND D ISCUSSIONS\nL558: In this section, experiments and comparisons were carried\nL559: \nL560: out to validate the effectiveness of the proposed approach,\nL561: \nL562: which is implemented in C++. In the following, ﬁrst, the testing\nL563: \nL564: data sets are introduced, and then, experiments and discussions\nL565: \nL566: are provided.\nL567: \nL568: A. Experimental Data Sets\nL569: \nL570: In our experiments, two data sets are used to evaluate the\nL571: \nL572: performance of the proposed approach. Each data set contains\nL573: \nL574: a high-resolution PAN image and a low spatial resolution MS\nL575: image composed of four different bands (blue, green, red, and\nL576: \nL577: near infrared). The ﬁrst data set, as shown in Fig. 2(a) and (b),\nL578: \nL579: was collected by the ZiYuan-3 (ZY-3) satellite on the area of\nL580: \nL581: Wuhan, China, which provides the PAN band at 2.1-m resolu-\nL582: \nL583: tion and the MS bands at 5.8-m resolution. The second data set,\nL584: \nL585: Fig. 2. Source images, registered images, and fused results of proposed\nL586: \nL587: approach for two data sets (displayed in a color-infrared manner). (a) ZY-3\nL588: PAN image, 1024 ×1024 pixels. (b) ZY-3 MS image, 745 ×747 pixels.\nL589: \nL590: (c) Registered image of JPSF for ZY-3 data set. (d) Fused image of JPSF for\nL591: \nL592: ZY-3 data set. (e) GeoEye-1 PAN image, 1024 ×1024 pixels. (f) GeoEye-1\nL593: \nL594: MS image, 503 ×460 pixels. (g) Registered image of JPSF for GeoEye-1 data\nL595: \nL596: set. (h) Fused image of JPSF for GeoEye-1 data set.\nL597: \nL598: as shown in Fig. 2(e) and (f), was collected by the GeoEye-1\nL599: \nL600: satellite on the area of Hobart, Australia, which provides the\nL601: PAN band at 0.5-m resolution and the MS bands at 2-m resolu-\nL602: \nL603: tion. Due to the insigniﬁcant variations of the terrain height in\nL604: \nL605: the small areas of the two data sets, the inﬂuence of the terrain\nL606: \nL607: elevation across the whole image is ignored in our experiments.\nL608: \nL609: B. Compared Methods\nL610: \nL611: To evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed approach, the\nL612: \nL613: proposed joint processes of registration and fusion are com-\nL614: \nL615: pared with the separate processes of registration and fusion. In\nL616: these experiments, the SVR method [17] is chosen to produce\nL617: \nL618: the fused image. The following registration methods are imple-\nL619: \nL620: mented and used for comparison.\nL621: \nL622: 1) SIFT registration method. SIFT matched features are ﬁrst\nL623: \nL624: obtained from PAN and MS images using nearest neigh-\nL625: \nL626: borhood distance ratio [18] and random sample consensus\nL627: \nL628: (RANSAC) [19], where the threshold of the distance ratio\nL629: \nL630: is 0.8. Then, the afﬁne transformation parameters are\nL631: \nL632: estimated by least square solution.\nL633: 2) The registration method combines SIFT features and\nL634: \nL635: normalized cross-correlation (NCC) control points (CPs)\nL636: \nL637: (SIFT+NCC) [20]. The details of extracting SIFT and\nL638: \nL639: NCC CPs, which are used to estimate the afﬁne trans-\nL640: \nL641: formation parameters, are described in [20].\nL642: \nL643: In these experiments, the principal component analysis is used\nL644: \nL645: to convert the MS image from multiband to single band in order\nL646: \nL647: to extract SIFT features from the MS image effectively [4].\nL648: \nL649: C. Experimental Results\nL650: In this section, we evaluate the registration accuracy and\nL651: \nL652: fusion performance of the proposed joint approach, respec-\nL653: \nL654: tively. To demonstrate the advantage of the proposed approach,\nL655: \nL656: joint processes of SIFT and SVR (JPSF) and joint processes\nL657: \nL658: of SIFT+NCC and SVR (JPSN) are compared with a separate\nL659: \nL660: scheme with SIFT and SVR (SSF) and a separate scheme\nL661: \nL662: with SIFT+NCC and SVR (SSN). In these experiments, we\nL663: \nL664: set N it = 100,ε=10\nL665: \nL666: −5,λ=0.1, and {n i,i=1,2,...,6}=\nL667: \nL668: {1,10−2,10−2,1,10−2,10−2}.\nL669: 470 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 3, MARCH 2015\nL670: \nL671: TAB L E I\nL672: \nL673: Q UANTITATIVE R ESULTS OF SSF, JPSF, SSN, AND JPSN M ETHODS IN\nL674: \nL675: ZY-3 AND G EO E YE-1 D ATA S ETS. CC, ERGAS, AND Q4 A RE U SED TO\nL676: \nL677: E VA L UA T E T H E F USION Q UALITY.RMSE AND RM S LOO A RE U SED\nL678: \nL679: TO E VA L UA T E T H E R EGISTRATION A CCURACY.∗:U NIT I S P IXEL\nL680: \nL681: Fig. 3. Color-coded image of ZY-3 normalized correlation coefﬁcients.\nL682: \nL683: (a) NCC result of SSF. (b) NCC result of JPSF. (c) NCC result of SSN.\nL684: (d) NCC result of JPSN.\nL685: \nL686: 1) Registration Accuracy Evaluation: Fig. 2(c) and (d)\nL687: \nL688: shows the registered and fused results of JPSF for the ZY-3\nL689: \nL690: data set, while Fig. 2(g) and (h) shows the registered and fused\nL691: \nL692: results of JPSF for the GeoEye-1 data set.\nL693: \nL694: To evaluate the accuracy of the registration process, we\nL695: \nL696: applied two different methods. One is based on the RMSE\nL697: \nL698: and RMS LOO [21]. Ten manually selected CPs, which were\nL699: \nL700: uniformly distributed in the PAN and original MS images,\nL701: were used to calculate the RMSE according to (7) shown at\nL702: \nL703: the bottom of the page, where (x i,y\nL704: \nL705: i)and (u i,v\nL706: \nL707: i)denote\nL708: \nL709: the coordinates of the i th manual CPs. For the SSF and SSN\nL710: \nL711: methods, matched feature points are used to compute the\nL712: \nL713: RMS LOO. For the JPSF and JPSN methods, we ﬁrst divided\nL714: \nL715: the PAN image into tiles of size 128 ×128 pixels, and then, we\nL716: \nL717: obtained a set of tie points based on the center of the tiles by\nL718: \nL719: the iterative optimization algorithm. Table I shows the RMSE\nL720: and RMS LOO results of all the methods. The other method is\nL721: \nL722: taken from [22]. Once the MS image had been registered, we\nL723: \nL724: ﬁrst generated the low spatial resolution synthetic PAN image,\nL725: \nL726: whose detailed explanation can be found in [17], and then, we\nL727: \nL728: computed the NCC over local windows (21 by 21) for each\nL729: \nL730: pixel between the PAN image and the synthetic PAN image.\nL731: \nL732: Due to the space limitation, only the NCC results of the ZY-3\nL733: \nL734: data set are shown in Fig. 3.\nL735: From Table I, it is clearly demonstrated that the image\nL736: \nL737: registration, using the proposed joint approach, results in lower\nL738: \nL739: RMSE and RMS LOO and can achieve the subpixel accuracy.\nL740: \nL741: By visual inspection of the NCC results, it would not be\nL742: \nL743: difﬁcult to ﬁnd that the NCC results of JPSF, SSN, and JPSN\nL744: \nL745: are higher than that of SSF across the whole image, particularly\nL746: \nL747: Fig. 4. Partial NCC analysis of SSF, JPSF, SSN, and JPSN methods in ZY-3\nL748: \nL749: data set.\nL750: on the right portion of the image. To investigate the quantitative\nL751: \n"
